{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Direct branch prediction\n\nFetch reads a 1,024-entry table of two-bit counters using bits [11:2] of the PC selected for the next clock edge. A registered bypass supplies the new counter on a simultaneous read and write to the same entry. Configuration initializes the counters to weakly not taken. Core reset preserves their history.\n\nLegal conditional branches use the counter's high bit. JAL predicts taken. Both require a word-aligned target in the 1 MiB instruction RAM. Fetch computes the absolute target once, and ID carries it in the existing immediate field. JALR resolves from forwarded rs1 in EX. On a direction mismatch, EX redirects to the successor it reports through RVFI. A data-memory hold delays that correction until EX advances.\n\nThe existing hazard controls already provided the required hold and flush behavior once EX qualified the correction. EX/MEM supplies the branch outcome and counter snapshot for training. The unused registered branch target was removed.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- All 53 single-retirement ALTOPS formal checks passed on the first run and on the final RTL.\n- All five cocotb suites passed. The pipeline tests cover a trained loop exit with dmem held and imem stalled on release, the exact sequence of accepted stores, a counter read coincident with training, and consecutive retirements on a JAL prediction hit.\n- Co-simulation passed for selftest and three random programs, each with and without stalls. With both stall generators enabled, CoreMark completed ten iterations in 4,188,685 bracketed cycles, down 302,800 cycles (6.74%) from baseline. All five CRCs matched and the UART reported “Correct operation validated.”\n\n## FPGA result\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 produced 5.172, 5.469 and 5.469 MHz. The median gives **13.06 CoreMark iterations/s**, 7.7% above the 12.12 baseline. Option 0 fell below the 5.3102 MHz threshold. Its worst path runs through operand forwarding and the EX ALU.\n\nThe timed design uses 12,592 logic cells, 482 registers and seven BSRAMs. The predictor accounts for one BSRAM. Relative to baseline, the design costs 280 additional logic cells and 22 additional registers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
