s in EX. Register the 18-bit quotient prefix, 18-bit partial remainder, low 14 dividend bits, divisor magnitude D, 34-bit 3D and sign flags in EX/MEM. Compute the final seven digits in MEM and capture the selected, sign-corrected result in MEM/WB. Two independent divides can issue on successive cycles. An immediate consumer of a nonzero destination uses the existing one-bubble load-use interlock and receives the result from MEM/WB. A dmem stall\n…[2414 of 5486 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a7b434131192ad22]…\nthe divisor, high-bit divisors whose 3D exceeds 32 bits, and seeded full-width operand pairs for all four operations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Include both divider modules in the pipeline build. Check immediate consumers in both source positions, independent back-to-back divides, writes to x0, and a divide held behind a stalled memory request. Check writeback values and consecutive channel-0 retirement orders.\nWrote and schema-validated the [micro_opt hypothesis](./cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r1s0.yaml). It splits RV32M division across the existing EX/MEM register, resolving nine radix-4 digits in EX and seven in MEM.\n\nA temporary prototype reached **23.47 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **93.7%** from 12.12. Median Gowin Fmax rose from 5.442 to 10.542 MHz with the same 4,491,485 bracketed cycles. The prototype passed CoreMark CRC validation, all 53 ALTOPS formal checks, and 14,740 arithmetic vectors.\n\nThe YAML records **+50%** expected fitness, the maximum allowed.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
