{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r1s0.yaml && ls -lh '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/81422bc2/tmp.aI8aAzncVi/core'\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\ntitle: Split RV32M division across the existing EX/MEM register\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline's 199.837 ns worst path crosses operand forwarding and the EX ALU on its way to data memory. A single-cycle radix-4 prototype reached 5.233 MHz. Splitting the same arithmetic across EX and MEM reached 10.901, 10.542 and 10.542 MHz across the three Gowin placements, versus a baseline median of 5.442 MHz. The split prototype passed the CoreMark CRC checks in the baseline's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles. Its median timing gives 23.47 iterations/s versus 12.12. The reported LUT4 count fell from 12,312 to 4,450.\nhypothesis: |\n  For DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU, compute nine radix-4 quotient digits from the forwarded operand magnitudes in EX. Register the 18-bit quotient prefix, 18-bit partial remainder, low 14 dividend bits, divisor magnitude D, 34-bit 3D and sign flags in EX/MEM. Compute the final seven digits in MEM and capture the selected, sign-corrected result in MEM/WB. This allows consecutive divides to issue on successive cycles when neither consumes the other's destination. A dependent instruction immediately following a divide uses the existing one-bubble load-use interlock and receives the result from MEM/WB. The existing dmem stall freezes the partial state with its instruction.\n  Each digit consumes two dividend bits and chooses among 0, D, 2D and 3D using parallel trial subtractions. Form multiples at 34-bit precision. Narrow each subtraction to the consumed dividend-prefix width, use its borrow plus the multiple's upper bits to determine fit, and select the difference for the largest fitting multiple. For a zero divisor every quotient digit is 3 and the remainder retains the dividend magnitude. Suppress quotient negation in this case and apply the dividend sign to the remainder. The unsigned magnitude of INT_MIN divided by one restores the required INT_MIN quotient and zero remainder for signed overflow. Route the specified ALTOPS formulas through the same pipeline boundary.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Factor division into combinational prefix and finish blocks. Use the same blocks in the standalone combinational ALU tests and place only the prefix in the core's EX divide path. Implement the ALTOPS subtract and XOR on the corresponding sides of the register.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Define the partial divider state and carry it in ex_mem_t. Share the decoded DIV/REM operation predicate across stages.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the divider prefix state alongside instruction metadata under the existing EX/MEM reset and hold enables.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Finish each divide from registered state and select its result into MEM/WB. Preserve the memory interface and the held WB data used by forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Mark valid DIV/REM producers alongside loads at the hazard unit's late-result input and exclude unfinished divide results from EX/MEM forwarding. Apply the same interlock in real arithmetic and ALTOPS builds.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: Extend exact-result checks with dividends bracketing multiples of the divisor, high-bit divisors whose 3D exceeds 32 bits, and seeded full-width operand pairs for all four operations.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check immediate divide consumers in both source positions, independent back-to-back divides, writes to x0, and a divide held behind a stalled memory request. Check writeback values and consecutive channel-0 retirement orders.\nls: cannot access '/srv/hwebench/tmp/800f6bc6/81422bc2/tmp.aI8aAzncVi/core': No such file or directory\n", "exit_code": 2, "status": "failed"}}
