{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat tools/eval/_slots.py'","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Host-wide slots for the harness's heavy evals (formal, Gowin builds).\n\nThree concurrent runs, each evaluating up to three slots, plus the agents'\nown self-checks, saturated the 20-core run host (load 20); formal checks then\nhit their wall-clock ceiling because of what other runs were doing, not\nbecause of the design (V2.1 campaign, first batch). With HARNESS_EVAL_SLOTS\nset, each eval holds one of that many flock'ed slots (in HARNESS_EVAL_LOCK_DIR,\noperator-only) for its duration, and its timeout starts once it holds one.\nUnset (agents' own `make timing`, tests): no limit. The variables use a prefix\nthe agent environment does not inherit (tools/agents/_runtime.py).\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport contextlib\nimport fcntl\nimport os\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\n@contextlib.contextmanager\ndef eval_slot(what: str = \"eval\"):\n    n = int(os.environ.get(\"HARNESS_EVAL_SLOTS\", \"0\") or 0)\n    lock_dir = os.environ.get(\"HARNESS_EVAL_LOCK_DIR\", \"\")\n    if n <= 0 or not lock_dir:\n        yield\n        return\n    d = Path(lock_dir)\n    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    started, told = time.time(), False\n    while True:\n        for i in range(n):\n            f = open(d / f\"slot{i}.lock\", \"w\")\n            try:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n            except BlockingIOError:\n                f.close()\n                continue\n            waited = time.time() - started\n            if waited > 60:\n                print(f\"  [eval] {what}: got host eval slot {i} after {waited:.0f}s\", flush=True)\n            try:\n                yield\n            finally:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n                f.close()\n            return\n        if not told:\n            print(f\"  [eval] {what}: waiting for a host eval slot ({n} busy)\", flush=True)\n            told = True\n        time.sleep(5)\n\n\ndef eval_jobs(default: str | None = None) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"make -j for a formal run holding a slot (HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS).\"\"\"\n    return os.environ.get(\"HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS\") or default\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct control transfers in the fetch cycle\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch always selects PC+4. Since IF/ID is combinational, each taken\n  branch or jump resolved in EX discards one younger fetch opportunity.\n  Between the CoreMark markers the reference ISS executes 294,425 taken\n  conditional branches and 49,367 JALs. Replaying the same instruction\n  trace through 64 two-bit counters gives 51,857 conditional misses.\n  With direct JAL prediction this removes about 292,000 redirect\n  opportunities from a 4,491,485-cycle run. The measured 156-cell timing\n  path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU, so the expected gain comes\n  from fewer cycles at similar Fmax.\nhypothesis: |\n  On an accepted fetch, use a 64-entry table of two-bit saturating\n  counters indexed by PC[7:2] to predict legal conditional branches.\n  A counter's high bit selects taken. Also select taken for JAL. Form the\n  target from the fetched B or J immediate and the current PC, and require\n  word alignment and a target in instruction RAM before selecting it.\n  Select PC+4 for all other fetches,\n  including JALR. Carry the actual fetch decision as one bit alongside\n  the instruction. Direct targets are fixed by the instruction, so EX can\n  detect a mistake by comparing this bit with the resolved taken decision.\n  On a mistake, redirect to the resolved successor, including PC+4 for a\n  predicted-taken branch that falls through. Train once per advancing\n  valid, legal conditional branch. Correct predictions let the younger\n  instruction enter ID/EX on the same edge that the branch advances.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Read direction by fetch PC. Predecode legal BRANCH funct3 and JAL\n      from raw imem_data and compute their signed PC-relative target.\n      Keep imem_addr driven by the PC register. Advance its prediction\n      only on fetch acceptance, with reset then EX correction taking\n      priority. Reset counters to weakly not taken and saturate updates\n      using the resolving branch's PC and outcome.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add a predicted_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch the prediction with its instruction, hold both on a stall and clear both on a flush.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compare predicted_taken with the resolved aligned transfer decision\n      using the existing forwarded operands. On a mismatch, send the same\n      architectural successor used for RVFI pc_next. Qualify correction\n      and training with in.valid and EX advance so a dmem hold defers\n      both until release. Preserve the existing target trap and link rules.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire resolved conditional-branch training back to IF and drive the\n      existing redirect and hazard flush inputs from the EX correction.\n      A correction must override an imem wait. A correct prediction must\n      preserve the fetched instruction subject to normal stalls.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
